<h2>Local SEO basics: showing up when it matters</h2>

<p>Local SEO is the practice of making your business visible in search results for location-based queries. If someone searches "accountant in Brisbane" or "dog groomer near me," local SEO determines whether you show up.</p>

<p>Beyond your Google Business Profile, here are the fundamentals:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Consistent NAP.</strong> Your Name, Address, and Phone number should be identical everywhere it appears online. Your website, your social profiles, your directory listings. Inconsistency confuses Google and hurts your ranking.</li>
  <li><strong>Local directory listings.</strong> Get listed on Yellow Pages, True Local, Yelp, and any industry-specific directories. These backlinks signal to Google that your business is legitimate and local.</li>
  <li><strong>Location pages on your website.</strong> If you serve multiple suburbs or regions, create a page for each one. "Plumbing services in [suburb name]" with content specific to that area.</li>
  <li><strong>Content that answers local questions.</strong> Blog posts like "How to choose a personal trainer in [city]" or "What to expect from a home inspection in [region]" attract search traffic from people in your area.</li>
</ul>

<p>Local SEO is not fast. It takes weeks to months to build momentum. But once it is working, it generates leads while you sleep. There is no ad spend involved and no daily management required.</p>

<h2>Paid vs. organic: when to spend and when to wait</h2>

<p>Organic strategies (SEO, social media, referrals) are slow to start but compound over time. Paid strategies (Google Ads, Facebook Ads) deliver results immediately but stop the moment you stop paying.</p>

<p>The smart approach is both. Use paid ads to generate leads while your organic presence builds. Then, as organic traffic grows, reduce your ad spend gradually. Think of paid ads as renting attention and organic as owning it.</p>

<p>If you are starting from zero and need leads now, a simple Google Ads campaign targeting "[your service] in [your area]" can start delivering enquiries within days. Budget as little as $10 to $20 per day. Send traffic to your dedicated landing page (not your homepage), and let your follow-up system handle the rest.</p>

<h2>Tracking where leads come from</h2>

<p>If you do not know where your leads come from, you cannot make informed decisions about where to spend your time and money. Set up tracking from day one.</p>

<p>At minimum, you need to know which channel each lead came from (Google, social media, referral, ad campaign), which leads converted into paying clients, and what the cost per lead and cost per client is for each channel.</p>

<p>This does not require complex analytics software. A CRM that tags leads by source and tracks them through your pipeline gives you everything you need. When you can see that Google Ads brings in leads at $30 each and Instagram brings them in at $80 each, the decision about where to invest becomes obvious.</p>

